eveloper/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065354/assets/ac75a1bca2b8012016657f4d1d88fdf1725c84b2a3ec48376bbcb4670f134ba3.png) we can write $\\mathbf{x}$ in terms of the vectors $\\mathbf c_1$ and $\\mathbf c_2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5{b}_{1}-2{b}_{2} \\\\  & =-2{b}_{2}+5{b}_{1} \\\\  & =2(-{b}_{2})-\\frac{5}{3}(-3{b}_{1}) \\\\  & =2{c}_{1}-\\frac{5}{3}{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065354/assets/d98192a7e16e605311ee2bb8791e6d86005c6d565ede80e68aaaeaf8097721a2.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065354/example-4827.json" "file_sha256" "8fdd30fad26ae01f6eb043cb9b5859783f0583382aae39e17ae848ca767adaf8"} {"question" "e-4828" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $\\mathcal{B}= \\left\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2,\\mathbf{b}_3 \\right\\}$ be a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065354/assets/1e4b0e4345ef6d0bd0b350cc973eaa7c15b654e76b06cd6f42740af0ab8fc877.png) What are the coordinates of $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}= \\left\\{ \\mathbf{c}_1, \\mathbf{c}_2, \\mathbf{c}_3 \\right\\},$ where $\\mathbf{c}_1 = -3\\mathbf{b}_2,$ $\\mathbf{c}_2=-\\mathbf{b}_3,$ and $\\mathbf{c}_3 = -4\\mathbf{b}_1?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065354/example-4828.json" "file_sha256" "7e833884f098816afc9f0c9c7bb6e8285afad801249bab33c0ab03a79a7a925f"} {"question" "e-4828" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065354/assets/a5e125038beb90ff4fd2b16bea5b08f83855c80f2a70a73ccfc15fa53fd45a30.png) we can write $\\mathbf{x}$ in terms of the vectors $\\mathbf c_1, \\mathbf c_2,$ and $\\mathbf c_3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-2{b}_{1}-3{b}_{2}+5{b}_{3} \\\\  & =-3{b}_{2}+5{b}_{3}-2{b}_{1} \\\\  & =(-3{b}_{2})-5(-{b}_{3})+\\frac{1}{2}(-4{b}_{1}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}-5{c}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065354/assets/f78654ee0612c6daa7e7a1fa26fb56ce0c3c94f6a7ba29805a569eac643946eb.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065354/example-4828.json" "file_sha256" "7e833884f098816afc9f0c9c7bb6e8285afad801249bab33c0ab03a79a7a925f"} {"question" "e-4829" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $\\mathcal{B}= \\left\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2 \\right\\}$ be a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065354/assets/aed9216ce4c536aad07f26eec0e0cb7e3032147a19c13e0a2d42925083c49518.png) What are the coordinates of $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}= \\left\\{ \\mathbf{c}_1, \\mathbf{c}_2 \\right\\},$ where $\\mathbf{c}_1 = \\mathbf{b}_2-\\mathbf{b}_1$ and $\\mathbf{c}_2 = 2\\mathbf{b}_1 - 3\\mathbf{b}_2?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065354/example-4829.json" "file_sha256" "5a192b764dbcb1f7ea4f00e4b9334032fae26643bbe7a77f6e42d421e9f3c16d"} {"question" "e-4829" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We are given that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}={b}_{2}-{b}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}=2{b}_{1}-3{b}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the $\\mathbf{b}$'s in terms of $\\mathbf{c}$'s. Multiplying the first equation by $2$ and adding the equations, we get \n\n$$\n2\\mathbf{c}_1+\\mathbf{c}_2 = -\\mathbf{b}_2\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\mathbf{b}_2 = -2\\mathbf{c}_1-\\mathbf{c}_2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}_1 = \\left( -2\\mathbf{c}_1-\\mathbf{c}_2 \\right) - \\mathbf{b}_1\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\mathbf{b}_1 = -3\\mathbf{c}_1-\\mathbf{c}_2.\n$$\n\nSince ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065354/assets/80531bde3f2ebb6314a3a297efa44e3e21a33d0b5f628c67c39ef0fa288f4c78.png) we can write the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ in terms of the vectors $\\mathbf c_1$ and $\\mathbf c_2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={b}_{1}-3{b}_{2} \\\\  & =(-3{c}_{1}-{c}_{2})-3(-2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}) \\\\  & =-3{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}+6{c}_{1}+3{c}_{2} \\\\  & =3{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065354/assets/f678c1ecc9878c30b04e891af9e6933f1edb42af8e155c3f4719838210e1cc1d.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065354/example-4829.json" "file_sha256" "5a192b764dbcb1f7ea4f00e4b9334032fae26643bbe7a77f6e42d421e9f3c16d"} {"question" "q-34769" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "reviewed_ma_solution" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065354/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "405259cf90ea0080bb537648ae921e87e177085b39c722ef1712a6535d0eae79" "reviewer_session" "01a118cc-89a0-7c90-9221-d6599ae13bca" "rationale" "Derived judgment: x=c1-2c2. Since b1=-c2 and b2=3c1, x=2b1+(1/3)b2. Thus the ordered B coordinates are [2,1/3], original option 4. The authentic solution and Correct grading confirm this; the stored bmatrix expression has identical coordinates and basis subscript." "bindings" {"problem" "8e3f0a4142f5b8795c24d1eb0eb1be9fdc00