1-5b2+2b3. Substituting c1=-2b2, c2=b3, and c3=-3b1 gives x=(5/2)c1+2c2-(4/3)c3. The ordered C coordinates are therefore [5/2,2,-4/3], original option 1. The authentic solution and Correct grading agree; the stored answer preserves these exact coordinates and basis subscript."}] "usage" {"e-4827" [] "q-34770" [] "e-4828" [] "q-34769" [] "e-4829" []} "basis" 2142 "retractions" [[17592186120292 :question/problem "Let $C = {c_{1}, c_{2}}$ be a basis of $R^{2}$ and $[x]_{C} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$. What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $B = {b_{1}, b_{2}}$, where $b_{1} =-c_{2}$ and $b_{2} = 3c_{1}$?"] [17592186120292 :question/answer-fields 17592186118227] [17592186120293 :question/problem "Let $B = {b_{1}, b_{2}, b_{3}}$ be a basis of $R^{3}$ and $[x]_{B} = \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$. What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C = {c_{1}, c_{2}, c_{3}}$, where $c_{1} =-2b_{2}$, $c_{2} = b_{3}$ and $c_{3} =-3b_{1}$?"] [17592186120293 :question/answer-fields 17592186118233] [17592186119561 :question/problem "Let $\\mathcal{B}= \\left\\{\\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2 \\right\\}$ be a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ What are the coordinates of $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}= \\left\\{\\mathbf{c}_1, \\mathbf{c}_2 \\right\\}$, where $\\mathbf{c}_1 = -\\mathbf{b}_2$ and $\\mathbf{c}_2 = -3\\mathbf{b}_1$?"] [17592186119561 :question/worked-solution "Since $\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ we can write $\\mathbf{x}$ in terms of the vectors $\\mathbf c_1$ and $\\mathbf c_2$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= 5b_{1} - 2b_{2} \\\\\n&=-2b_{2} + 5b_{1} \\\\\n&= 2(-b_{2}) - \\frac{5}{3}(-3b_{1}) \\\\\n&= 2c_{1} - \\frac{5}{3}c_{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186119562 :question/problem "Let $\\mathcal{B}= \\left\\{\\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2,\\mathbf{b}_3 \\right\\}$ be a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$ What are the coordinates of $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}= \\left\\{\\mathbf{c}_1, \\mathbf{c}_2, \\mathbf{c}_3 \\right\\}$, where $\\mathbf{c}_1 = -3\\mathbf{b}_2$, $\\mathbf{c}_2=-\\mathbf{b}_3$, and $\\mathbf{c}_3 = -4\\mathbf{b}_1$?"] [17592186119562 :question/worked-solution "Since $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$ we can write $\\mathbf{x}$ in terms of the vectors $\\mathbf c_1, \\mathbf c_2$, and $\\mathbf c_3$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= -2b_{1} - 3b_{2} + 5b_{3} \\\\\n&=-3b_{2} + 5b_{3} - 2b_{1} \\\\\n&= (-3b_{2}) - 5(-b_{3}) + \\frac{1}{2}(-4b_{1}) \\\\\n&= c_{1} - 5c_{2} + \\frac{1}{2}c_{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186119563 :question/problem "Let $\\mathcal{B}= \\left\\{\\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2 \\right\\}$ be a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$ What are the coordinates of $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}= \\left\\{\\mathbf{c}_1, \\mathbf{c}_2 \\right\\}$, where $\\mathbf{c}_1 = \\mathbf{b}_2-\\mathbf{b}_1$ and $\\mathbf{c}_2 = 2\\mathbf{b}_1 - 3\\mathbf{b}_2$?"] [17592186119563 :question/worked-solution "We are given that\n\n$$\n{c_{1} = b_{2} - b_{1}; c_{2} = 2b_{1} - 3b_{2}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the $\\mathbf{b}$'s in terms of $\\mathbf{c}$'s. Multiplying the first equation by $2$ and adding the equations, we get\n\n$$\n2\\mathbf{c}_1+\\mathbf{c}_2 = -\\mathbf{b}_2 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\mathbf{b}_2 = -2\\mathbf{c}_1-\\mathbf{c}_2\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}_1 = \\left(-2\\mathbf{c}_1-\\mathbf{c}_2 \\right) - \\mathbf{b}_1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\mathbf{b}_1 = -3\\mathbf{c}_1-\\mathbf{c}_2\n$$\n\nSince $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$ we can write the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ in terms of the vectors $\\mathbf c_1$ and $\\mathbf c_2$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= b_{1} - 3b_{2} \\\\\n&= (-3c_{1} - c_{2}) - 3(-2c_{1} - c_{2}) \\\\\n&=-3c_{1} - c_{2} + 6c_{1} + 3c_{2} \\\\\n&= 3c_{1} + 2c_{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186118228 17592186118229 17592186118230 17592186118231 17592186118232 17592186118234 17592186118235 17592186118236 17592186118237 17592186118238] "immutable_fields" [[17592186118227 :answer-field/correct] [17592186118227 :answer-field/id] [17592186118227 :answer-field/key] [17592186118227 :answer-field/type] [17592186118233 :answer-field/correct] [17592186118233 :answer-field/id] [17592186118233 :answer-field/key] [17592186118233 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
